description:
Write the arithmetic behind lesson 4.01's "paint only what changed":

```cpp
struct RECT { long left, top, right, bottom; };

RECT exposedAfterScroll(RECT client, int dy);                 // the strip to repaint after ScrollWindowEx(0, dy)
void rowsToPaint(RECT paint, int scrollY, int rowHeight, int rowCount, int& first, int& last);

class BackBuffer
{
public:
    bool ensure(int width, int height);      // true if the bitmap had to be (re)created
    int width() const;
    int height() const;
};
```

- `exposedAfterScroll`: scrolling the content by `dy` pixels (negative = content moves up) leaves a strip of `|dy|` pixels at the bottom (dy < 0) or top (dy > 0) to repaint. If `|dy|` is at least the client height, the whole client area is exposed. `dy == 0` exposes nothing: `{0, 0, 0, 0}`.
- `rowsToPaint`: rows of `rowHeight` pixels are laid out from document y = 0; the window shows document y `scrollY` at its top. Give the first and last row (inclusive) that intersect the paint rectangle, clamped to `0 .. rowCount - 1`; if none, `first = 0` and `last = -1`.
- `BackBuffer::ensure` keeps the current bitmap if it is at least as large as asked on both axes. Otherwise it is re-created with each side rounded **up** to a multiple of 256, and never smaller than before on either axis (so a window that grows and shrinks does not keep re-creating it).

